type Product = {
  name: string;
  heroUrl: string;
  related: { id: string; name: string; imageUrl: string }[];
};

export function ProductPage({ product }: { product: Product }) {
  return (
    <main>
      <section className="hero" style={{ backgroundImage: `url(${product.heroUrl})` }} />
      <h1>{product.name}</h1>
      <ul>
        {product.related.map((p) => (
          <li key={p.id}>
            <img src={p.imageUrl} alt={p.name} />
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </main>
  );
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Audit: (1) The hero is a CSS background-image, so it is only discovered after the stylesheet is downloaded and parsed; an image declared in the HTML is found during the initial parse. That adds resource-load delay to the likely LCP element. (2) There is a single heroUrl for all devices, so mobile downloads desktop-sized bytes, inflating resource-load duration for the exact segment that regressed. (3) The related-product images have no width or height, so each arrival moves the content below it: a direct CLS source. (4) The related images are eager, so their requests compete for bandwidth with the hero during the window that determines LCP. Refactor:

```tsx
type Product = {
  name: string;
  heroUrl: string;
  heroMobileUrl: string;
  related: { id: string; name: string; imageUrl: string }[];
};

export function ProductPage({ product }: { product: Product }) {
  return (
    <main>
      <picture>
        <source srcSet={product.heroMobileUrl} media="(max-width: 640px)" />
        <img src={product.heroUrl} width={1280} height={720} alt={product.name} />
      </picture>
      <h1>{product.name}</h1>
      <ul>
        {product.related.map((p) => (
          <li key={p.id}>
            <img src={p.imageUrl} width={320} height={240} loading="lazy" alt={p.name} />
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </main>
  );
}
```

The hero is now an img in the HTML (early discovery, eager by default) with responsive sources and intrinsic dimensions; the related images are deferred with loading="lazy" and reserve their space. Then verify: confirm the LCP element in field data, check the waterfall for when the hero request starts, and watch field CLS for the route, because the refactor is only proven when the metrics move.

code.code:
type Row = { id: string; label: string; score: number };

const yieldToMain = () => new Promise<void>((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 0));

export async function applyFilter(rows: Row[], term: string) {
  const list = document.getElementById("results") as HTMLUListElement;
  const counter = document.getElementById("count") as HTMLElement;
  const button = document.getElementById("apply") as HTMLButtonElement;

  button.disabled = true;
  list.replaceChildren();

  let matched = 0;
  for (const row of rows) {
    if (!row.label.toLowerCase().includes(term)) continue;
    matched++;
    const li = document.createElement("li");
    li.textContent = row.label;
    list.appendChild(li);
    await yieldToMain();
  }

  counter.textContent = `${matched} results`;
  list.scrollTop = 0;
  button.disabled = false;
  return matched;
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Prediction: the `button.disabled = true` write and the start of the loop land in the same task as the click, so the disabled state cannot paint before the work begins; the user presses the button and sees nothing change for roughly a second, which is exactly the input delay that shows up as a ~700 ms INP for that target. After that, the code awaits a `setTimeout` per matched row, so a 2,000-match run creates thousands of macrotasks; once timers nest past the fifth level the browser clamps them to ~4 ms, so the tail alone adds seconds and the list repopulates in visible flickers while the counter still reads the old value.

The defects, in order of impact:

1. **No acknowledgement before the work.** A single `await` after setting the busy state is what lets the browser paint it, in a separate task.
2. **Yield granularity is per row, not per budget.** `await` on every iteration makes the operation slower, not just responsive: the yield is unconditional and the timer fallback is the most expensive yield available.
3. **The cheap filter and the expensive DOM work are interleaved.** Matching `label.toLowerCase().includes(term)` over 20k rows is a few milliseconds; creating and inserting 20k list items is the real cost, and it is being done one node at a time against a live list.
4. **No progress feedback and a full clear up front.** `replaceChildren()` empties the list before anything replaces it, so the layout collapses and the user stares at an empty box; the counter is only written at the end.

Refactor:

```ts
type Row = { id: string; label: string; score: number };

const yieldToMain = (): Promise<void> =>
  (globalThis as any).scheduler?.yield?.() ??
  new Promise<void>((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 0));

export async function applyFilter(rows: Row[], term: string) {
  const list = document.getElementById("results") as HTMLUListElement;
  const counter = document.getElementById("count") as HTMLElement;
  const button = document.getElementById("apply") as HTMLButtonElement;

  // 1. acknowledge first, then let the browser paint it in its own task
  button.disabled = true;
  list.setAttribute("aria-busy", "true");
  counter.textContent = "Filtering…";
  await yieldToMain();

  // 2. the cheap pass stays synchronous and never touches the DOM
  const needle = term.toLowerCase();
  const matched = rows.filter((row) => row.label.toLowerCase().includes(needle));

  // 3. render in time-budgeted batches, many rows per task
  list.replaceChildren();
  const fragment = document.createDocumentFragment();
  let batchStart = performance.now();

  for (const row of matched) {
    const li = document.createElement("li");
    li.textContent = row.label;
    fragment.appendChild(li);

    if (performance.now() - batchStart > 50) {
      list.appendChild(fragment); // moves the children and empties the fragment
      await yieldToMain();
      batchStart = performance.now();
    }
  }
  list.appendChild(fragment);

  counter.textContent = `${matched.length} results`;
  list.removeAttribute("aria-busy");
  button.disabled = false;
  list.scrollTop = 0;
  return matched.length;
}
```

Why this is the right shape: `scheduler.yield()` hands control back with the work's continuation at higher priority than a timer, so an input that arrives mid-run is processed before the next batch continues (https://web.dev/articles/optimize-long-tasks). The 50 ms budget keeps each task under the long-task threshold instead of promising one task per row, which also removes the nested-timer clamp from the hot path. Filtering first and rendering second keeps the synchronous pass a few milliseconds and leaves only DOM construction to be batched, and `DocumentFragment` means one insertion per batch instead of one per node, so the list does not recalculate layout 2,000 times.

The verification is two-sided. In a trace of the same interaction, the long task should be gone: several short tasks, a paint between them, the disabled state painted before the first batch, and the input handled mid-run. In the field, INP for that button's target element should drop while LCP and the row count stay unchanged. Only if the predicate itself became expensive (fuzzy scoring instead of `includes`) does the filtering move to a Worker, with the results posted back in chunks and the DOM work still on the main thread.
